editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Keep the inclusive endpoints of the rightmost palindrome for each parity. For a center inside that interval, mirror its center and initialize its radius to the smaller of the mirror radius and the right-boundary allowance. Extend with explicit character comparisons. This can cross the right boundary only O(n) times in total. For odd length use its middle character. For even length use the right-hand middle index, whose even radius counts matched pairs. A query is palindromic exactly when its two endpoints fit inside the precomputed radius of its own center and parity.

With inclusive window [l,r] and zero-based center i, the mirrored odd center is l+r−i. For an even center indexed by the character immediately right of the gap, it is l+r−i+1. In both cases cap the copied radius at r−i+1, since the window gives no information beyond r.

## Walk through the sample
In abacabba, [5,8] spells abba. Its even center is the gap before position 7; two matching pairs reach both endpoints. By contrast [3,6] spells acab, whose outer characters already disagree.

## Complexity
When a mirror stops strictly inside the window, the next comparison fails by symmetry. Otherwise every successful extension advances the known right boundary. Each center has constant setup and at most one failed comparison, so preprocessing takes O(n) time and space. Each query takes O(1).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

vector<int> radii(const string &s, bool even) {
    int n = s.size(), l = 0, r = -1;
    vector<int> d(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int k = i > r ? (even ? 0 : 1) : min(d[l + r - i + even], r - i + 1);
        while (i - k - (even ? 1 : 0) >= 0 && i + k < n &&
               s[i - k - (even ? 1 : 0)] == s[i + k])
            ++k;
        d[i] = k;
        if (i + k - 1 > r) {
            l = i - k + (even ? 0 : 1);
            r = i + k - 1;
        }
    }
    return d;
}
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    string s;
    int q;
    cin >> s >> q;
    auto odd = radii(s, false), even = radii(s, true);
    while (q--) {
        int l, r;
        cin >> l >> r;
        --l;
        --r;
        int length = r - l + 1, center = l + length / 2;
        bool yes = length % 2 ? odd[center] >= length / 2 + 1 : even[center] >= length / 2;
        cout << (yes ? "YES\n" : "NO\n");
    }
}
